==Old Norse==

===Etymology 1===
From {{proto|Germanic|hwar|lang=non}}, whence also Old English {{term|hwær}} (English {{term|where}}), Dutch {{term|waar}}, Old High German {{term|wara|lang=goh}}, Gothic {{term|𐍈𐌰𐍂|tr=hwar|sc=Goth}}.

====Adverb====
{{non-adv|-}}

# [[where]]

=====Descendants=====
{{top2}}
* Danish: [[hvor#Danish|hvor]]
* Faroese: [[#Faroese|hvar]]
* Icelandic: [[#Icelandic|hvar]]
{{mid2}}
* Norwegian (bokmål): [[hvor#Norwegian|hvor]]
* Norwegian (nynorsk): [[kvar]], [[kor]]
* Swedish: [[var#Swedish|var]]
{{bottom}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{proto|Germanic|hwaz|lang=non}}, whence also Old English {{term|hwa|lang=ang}} (English {{term|who}}), Old High German {{term|hwer|lang=goh}} (German {{term|wer|lang=de}}), Gothic {{term|𐍈𐌰𐍃|tr=hwas|sc=Goth}}.

====Pronoun====
'''*hvar''' (''nominative form uncertain as the word is abbreviated in manuscripts, dative'' '''[[hveim]]'''; ''neuter'' '''[[hvat#Old Norse|hvat]]''', ''dative'' '''[[hví#Old Norse|hví]]''', ''genitive'' '''[[hves]]''')

# [[who]]

